Klaus Kassner <Klaus.Kassner@physik.uni-magdeburg.de> wrote:

[cat quits a game]
>Then I read in one of the posts following your story that you can quit
>the game by typing "<alt>q" rather than "#q".  I had not been aware of
>that - I knew that in the old times "<shift>q" was what you typed for
>quitting, but this had to be changed to liberate the letter "Q" for the
>command "quiver", and since I have so often seen the message "Use #quit
>to quit the game", I somehow believed this is the only way now.  

Use the option "suppress_alert:3.3.1" to get rid of the warning.

>But I also think that the DevTeam should simply remove the "shortcut"
>to quitting that is provided by using the "<alt>" key instead of the "#"
>one.  From my estimates it transpires that with 95% probability it was
>the "<alt>q" command that ended your game rather than "#quit".  This
>simple measure would save 19 out of 20 games that otherwise get lost by
>accidents leading to a sequence of many random keys to be sent to the
>computer.  Second, I think that the default behaviour should not be
>triggered only if you type one of the equivalents of "n" but always if
>you type anything but "y".  This would reduce the probability of
>disaster by another factor of about 10.  Whether a paranoia patch,
>requiring the full answer of "yes" to be typed to the question whether
>one would like to quit, would then still be necessary, is a question of
>personal taste.  

I think we should leave trampling cats ot ouf consideration. Many
accidental deaths or mishaps result from pressing a choice key too fast
(eating something from the ground instead of an item in slot y; drinking
a wrong potion instead of the one that happened to be in the same slot).
Indeed, I find that when I issue a command, I often do not wait to read
the confirmation message but hit the appropriate key right away. The
paranoia patch prevents accidental quitting from those events.
(Additionally, it lowers the chance of quitting by hitting random keys
from miniscule to negligible.)

